<Base
  title="ReadFluent"
  description="Read the original. Understand the translation. A bilingual e-book reader for iPhone, iPhone Duo, and iPad."
>
  <article>
    <header class="post-header">
      <p class="kicker">ReadFluent</p>
      <h1>Read the original. Understand the translation.</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        ReadFluent is a bilingual e-book reader for iPhone, iPhone Duo, and iPad. You keep the
        book in the language it was written in, and a translation of the page is there when you
        need it.
      </p>

      <h2>Your book, your shelf</h2>
      <p>
        Import an EPUB you already own. The file, its title, author, and cover stay in the app on
        your device. The library is not uploaded to a server and is not synced through iCloud.
      </p>

      <h2>Original and translation together</h2>
      <p>
        On a phone, Translate switches between the original page and the translated page. On a
        wide landscape window, such as an iPad or an open iPhone Duo, the original and the
        translation sit on facing pages. You choose the language you are reading toward. If that
        language is not installed, the system may ask to download it.
      </p>

      <h2>A place you can return to</h2>
      <p>
        A bookmark saves the passage, not a screen position, and opens those lines again later.
        The page can be warm white, sepia, or charcoal. That choice stays on the device.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/support/readfluent">Support</a>
        ·
        <a href="/privacy/readfluent">Privacy policy</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
  <a class="back" href="/blog/readfluent">More about ReadFluent</a>
</Base>
